nodecode-memory-shaper is a rewrite of ockentap's ockentap/hermes-dynamic-memory:

  https://github.com/ockentap/hermes-dynamic-memory
  commit 5adc0853e364cb3965d389e5486e6a001ffbba87
  Apache License 2.0, Copyright (c) 2026 ockentap (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's core promise, kept in the same words -- a memory index is a flat table of one line per subject, five to twenty comma-separated keywords and a Unicode arrow pointing at a bare `.md` name, the verbose sentences live in that file, and a small index rides in front of the model while the detail stays on disk. The validation rules came over as the original stated them: the Unicode arrow only (an ASCII arrow is refused with its own message), the keyword count, a bare `.md` target with a relative `../` form allowed, prose refused by its comma-to-word ratio, and a line past 140 characters sent to a topic file named after its first words. The refusals keep the original's fix-it wording rather than a bare error. They are the memory_index tool (add, route, lint, show, rewrite) and the /memory-shaper command.

What was left out, and why: the original was a gate on another harness's memory tool and file writes -- a pre_tool_call hook plus a tool_execution middleware that refused write_file, patch, shell redirects, tee and sed -i aimed at MEMORY.md, protected the index header and the template line, and let a scripted rewrite through an environment variable or a sentinel file in the harness's state folder. Nodecode's memory writes go through its own definitions and its own tool, and the index here is a file this cell owns, so there is no second writer to intercept: the shape is checked where the entry is written, the rewrite is allowed by a section setting instead of a sentinel file, and lint reports a hand edit after the fact. Also left out: the harness-specific MEMORY.md/USER.md routing and the section-sign separator reasoning, the template placeholder auto-cleanup and its shipped example lines, the detection of python scripts writing the index, and the optional core patch the repository ships for the other harness.
